{
  "abstract": "For every Borel probability law on the real line that is symmetric about zero and satisfies 0 < a <= |X| <= b almost surely, the support ratio b/a <= sqrt(3) + sqrt(2) guarantees E exp(t X) <= exp(t^2 E X^2 / 2) for every real t. The inequality is strict for nonzero t, and the actual variance is the optimal quadratic proxy. An explicit symmetric law on {-R,-1,1,R} violates the bound near zero for each larger ratio R. In the normalization 3-sqrt(6) <= X^2 <= 3+sqrt(6), the development also establishes the sharp eighth-order MGF gap with its optimal interiority coefficient, identifies the unique law with Gaussian fourth-moment contact, and proves the sharp global Wasserstein bound for the squared law at every feasible fourth-cumulant deficit, including attainment. The proof combines a convex endpoint reduction, control of the entire moment tail, exact rational Bernstein certificates, and an explicit affine transport coupling.",
  "authors": [
    {
      "name": "JD Jones"
    }
